    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Disaster_Response_Logistics\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)  # Set time limit to 300 seconds\n\n    # Data\n    depots = list(range(3))  # D0..D2\n    units = list(range(3))  # U0..U2\n    supplies = list(range(3))  # S0..S2\n    days = list(range(1))  # Day0..Day0\n\n    demands = [[[41.9], [11.62], [14.5]], [[10.14], [15.83], [32.97]], [[27.87], [14.74], [12.08]]]\n    depot_supplies = [[[25.33], [15.01], [17.23]], [[31.38], [14.39], [27.17]], [[23.28], [13.2], [27.09]]]\n    transport_costs = [[2.11, 3.53, 3.75], [2.56, 2.82, 2.95], [3.67, 2.23, 2.18]]\n    risk_costs = [[150.36, 113.06, 129.94], [163.55, 85.33, 164.01], [151.63, 114.74, 174.99]]\n    supply_volumes = [0.68, 1.47, 1.03]\n    penalty_costs = [76.79, 96.62, 74.46]\n    vehicle_cost = 10.0\n    vehicle_capacity = 100.0\n    total_vehicles = 20\n\n    # Decision variables\n    # Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Amount of supply s from depot d to unit u on day t\n    flows = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                for s in supplies:\n                    flows[(d, u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Shortage of supply s at unit u on day t\n    slacks = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                slacks[(u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Number of vehicles on route d->u on day t\n    vehicles = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                vehicles[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.INTEGER, name=f\"Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Route d->u is active on day t (binary)\n    routes = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                routes[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize total cost (transport + penalty + vehicle + risk)\n    obj = quicksum(\n        transport_costs[d][u] * flows[(d, u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        penalty_costs[s] * slacks[(u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        vehicle_cost * vehicles[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        risk_costs[d][u] * routes[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Depot capacity constraints: Total outflow <= supply\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for u in units) <= depot_supplies[d][s][t],\n                    name=f\"DepotCap_D{d}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Demand constraints: Inflow + slack >= demand\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for d in depots) + slacks[(u, s, t)] >= demands[u][s][t],\n                    name=f\"Demand_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Vehicle capacity constraints: Total volume <= vehicles * capacity\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(supply_volumes[s] * flows[(d, u, s, t)] for s in supplies) <= vehicle_capacity * vehicles[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"VehCap_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Route activation constraints: vehicles <= total_vehicles * route\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    vehicles[(d, u, t)] <= total_vehicles * routes[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"RouteAct_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Fleet size constraints: Total vehicles <= total_vehicles\n    for t in days:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(vehicles[(d, u, t)] for d in depots for u in units) <= total_vehicles,\n            name=f\"FleetSize_Day{t}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Situation:\nA severe weather system has disrupted road networks across the operational area. Flooding, landslides, and damaged bridges constrain overland movement. Logistics Hub Alpha, Supply Depot Bravo, and Central Warehouse Charlie hold critical commodities awaiting dispatch. Priority receiving elements include Field Hospital Delta, Shelter Cluster Echo, and Relief Company Foxtrot. Ground convoys remain the primary mode. Several corridors require security escort and engineer route clearance due to instability and interference risks. Aviation support is limited and reserved for medical evacuation. All distribution will occur within the current operational window.\n\nMission:\nAllocate vehicles and distribute lifesaving supplies from the depots to the receiving units to meet prioritized requirements while minimizing total operational cost (transport, shortage penalties, vehicle deployment, and fixed route security risk costs for activated routes).\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Commodities include medical items, safe water-related supplies, and emergency rations. Each supply type has an associated volume factor used to calculate vehicle loading.\n- Distribution flows from each depot to each unit are constrained by available stocks at origin. No depot may dispatch more of a supply than it holds.\n- At each receiving unit, demand by supply type must be met; controlled shortfalls are permitted when operationally necessary and will incur a shortage penalty. Slack represents shortfall to be minimized given cost-risk trade-offs.\n- Vehicles are standardized and subject to a load limit defined by capacity and supply volumes. Vehicle allocations are integer; fractional vehicles are not possible.\n- Route activation is required to move any vehicles on a depot-to-unit corridor. Activating a route incurs a fixed Risk Cost for the period, representing security escort, route clearance, and overwatch, regardless of the number of vehicles dispatched on that route.\n- Vehicles can only use active routes. Total vehicles across all routes are limited by fleet availability for the period.\n- Transport costs vary by route and supply. Vehicle deployment cost applies per vehicle used.\n- Operational approach:\n  - Prioritize deliveries that enable life-saving care and stabilize water and shelter needs.\n  - Consolidate loads to reduce the number of activated routes when feasible, balancing per-unit transport costs against fixed route security overhead.\n  - Avoid high-risk corridors unless necessary to meet critical gaps or to relieve isolated populations.\n  - Utilize depots with favorable stock and access to reduce transshipment and turnaround times.\n  - Maintain a reserve of vehicles to respond to evolving incidents and medical surge requests.\n- Coordination:\n  - Movement Control will schedule convoys, deconflict route windows, and ensure escorts are assigned to any activated corridor.\n  - Security Task Force and Engineer teams will confirm route status before activation and coordinate clearance assets where required.\n  - Receiving units will prepare offload areas and confirm receipt against manifests to minimize dwell time.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\nUnit Demands (Units x Supplies):\nUnit\\Supply | S 0 | S 1 | S 2\n-----------------------------\nU 0        |  41.90 |  11.62 |  14.50\nU 1        |  10.14 |  15.83 |  32.97\nU 2        |  27.87 |  14.74 |  12.08\nDepot Supplies (Depots x Supplies):\nDepot\\Supply | S 0 | S 1 | S 2\n------------------------------\nD 0        |  25.33 |  15.01 |  17.23\nD 1        |  31.38 |  14.39 |  27.17\nD 2        |  23.28 |  13.20 |  27.09\nTransport Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2\n----------------------------\nD 0        |   2.11 |   3.53 |   3.75\nD 1        |   2.56 |   2.82 |   2.95\nD 2        |   3.67 |   2.23 |   2.18\nRisk Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2\n----------------------------\nD 0        | 150.36 | 113.06 | 129.94\nD 1        | 163.55 |  85.33 | 164.01\nD 2        | 151.63 | 114.74 | 174.99\nPenalty Costs per Unit Shortage: [Supply S0: 76.79, Supply S1: 96.62, Supply S2: 74.46]\nVolume per Supply Unit: [Supply S0: 0.68, Supply S1: 1.47, Supply S2: 1.03]\nFleet Parameters: Cost 10.0, Capacity 100.0, Total 20",
    "LLM_description_template": "Situation:\nA severe weather system has disrupted road networks across the operational area. Flooding, landslides, and damaged bridges constrain overland movement. Logistics Hub Alpha, Supply Depot Bravo, and Central Warehouse Charlie hold critical commodities awaiting dispatch. Priority receiving elements include Field Hospital Delta, Shelter Cluster Echo, and Relief Company Foxtrot. Ground convoys remain the primary mode. Several corridors require security escort and engineer route clearance due to instability and interference risks. Aviation support is limited and reserved for medical evacuation. All distribution will occur within the current operational window.\n\nMission:\nAllocate vehicles and distribute lifesaving supplies from the depots to the receiving units to meet prioritized requirements while minimizing total operational cost (transport, shortage penalties, vehicle deployment, and fixed route security risk costs for activated routes).\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Commodities include medical items, safe water-related supplies, and emergency rations. Each supply type has an associated volume factor used to calculate vehicle loading.\n- Distribution flows from each depot to each unit are constrained by available stocks at origin. No depot may dispatch more of a supply than it holds.\n- At each receiving unit, demand by supply type must be met; controlled shortfalls are permitted when operationally necessary and will incur a shortage penalty. Slack represents shortfall to be minimized given cost-risk trade-offs.\n- Vehicles are standardized and subject to a load limit defined by capacity and supply volumes. Vehicle allocations are integer; fractional vehicles are not possible.\n- Route activation is required to move any vehicles on a depot-to-unit corridor. Activating a route incurs a fixed Risk Cost for the period, representing security escort, route clearance, and overwatch, regardless of the number of vehicles dispatched on that route.\n- Vehicles can only use active routes. Total vehicles across all routes are limited by fleet availability for the period.\n- Transport costs vary by route and supply. Vehicle deployment cost applies per vehicle used.\n- Operational approach:\n  - Prioritize deliveries that enable life-saving care and stabilize water and shelter needs.\n  - Consolidate loads to reduce the number of activated routes when feasible, balancing per-unit transport costs against fixed route security overhead.\n  - Avoid high-risk corridors unless necessary to meet critical gaps or to relieve isolated populations.\n  - Utilize depots with favorable stock and access to reduce transshipment and turnaround times.\n  - Maintain a reserve of vehicles to respond to evolving incidents and medical surge requests.\n- Coordination:\n  - Movement Control will schedule convoys, deconflict route windows, and ensure escorts are assigned to any activated corridor.\n  - Security Task Force and Engineer teams will confirm route status before activation and coordinate clearance assets where required.\n  - Receiving units will prepare offload areas and confirm receipt against manifests to minimize dwell time.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\n{DEMANDS}\n{DEPOT_SUPPLIES}\n{TRANSPORT_COSTS}\n{RISK_COSTS}\n{PENALTY_COSTS}\n{SUPPLY_VOLUMES}\nFleet Parameters: Cost {VEHICLE_COST}, Capacity {VEHICLE_CAPACITY}, Total {TOTAL_VEHICLES}"
